# NO SOURCE ARCHIVES IN THE TAR OF /usr -- updatebuild reads this and dissolves every one,
# idempotently.  Source members are extracted in place, in a directory named
# as the archive was, under the repository's member names (u_ where members
# collide by case, matching build/casenames' rule); the archive file is then
# deleted.  Object members and all-object archives are deleted outright:
# "there should be no source archives, and archive objects cannot be included
# in the tar" -- the link libraries the kernel reads (sys/lib/*.a) rebuild
# from source on the next ipnxbuild, and the object-only tape libraries are
# recoverable from v10.tar.  ORDER, written from ar t at extraction, keeps
# the one thing a directory cannot: the member order.
#
# FOLDERS CANNOT HAVE EXTENSIONS (her rule): the extraction directory is the
# archive's bare stem -- tek.c.a becomes tek/, test.a becomes test/.
#
# ops: x archive dirname (extract members into dirname/, delete archive)
#      m dirname/member new (rename member to its collision-safe name)
#      d dirname/member (delete an object member after extraction)
#      r archive (delete an all-object archive)
#
# THE TAR OF /usr WIDENED THE SCOPE.  When ipnx.tar became a tar of /usr the
# beside-src trees came into it, and blit, jerq and 630 carry 29 archives of
# their own.  The classification is the repository's: every one of the 29 is
# already dissolved there, so counting .o among a directory's members settles
# it -- 21 directories are all-object (blit/lib's eight cross libraries,
# 630/lib's six, and seven in-tree products; every one rebuildable from source
# that sits in the same tree, and every byte recoverable from the tapes) and
# get r rows; 8 hold no objects at all (the jplot source containers and the
# newspec font data) and get x rows.  No member of the 8 collides by case, so
# no m rows.
